  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Focused on the exposure, Nagiev confronts Vika about her fake excuse and forces her to pay for the trip herself. Let's listen closely to Vika and Dmitry Nagiyev's exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Пожалуй, добрый день, Вика.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Почему, Вик, на выставку не улетели?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Так ее же отменили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я звонила своим друзьям.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Вам же звонил там какой-то предприниматель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, звонил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Сейчас скажем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Александр Дель Пьеро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сказал, что выставка отменяется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "С московского телефона звонят?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Да, вот номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Набирайте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Я бы вас уволил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "А так вы просто летите на выставку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "И за свой счет.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around The Exposure line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, Дмитрий Владимирович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning, Dmitry Vladimirovich.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'good' directly with 'morning' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Пожалуй, добрый день, Вика.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I would say it is afternoon, Vika.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'good' directly with 'day', combining 'good' with 'day'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Почему, Вик, на выставку не улетели?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why didn't you fly out to the exhibition?' The phrasing opens with 'why' before bringing in 'exhibition' and 'to fly away' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Так ее же отменили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It was cancelled.' Vika delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to cancel'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я звонила своим друзьям.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I called my contacts.' Vika chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'called' for the ongoing action, followed by 'friend'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Вам же звонил там какой-то предприниматель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Some businessman called you, right?' Nagiyev chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'called' for the ongoing action, followed by 'there' and 'entrepreneur'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who?' Vika delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'what a'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, звонил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, he called.' Vika delivers a punchy conversational reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'called'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Сейчас скажем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's see.' Nagiyev puts the word for 'now' together with 'we'll say' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Александр Дель Пьеро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alexander Del Piero.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'Alexander' directly with 'Del', combining 'Alexander' with 'Del' and 'Pierrot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сказал, что выставка отменяется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He said the exhibition was cancelled.' Vika uses the perfective verb meaning 'said' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'exhibition' and 'is canceled'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "С московского телефона звонят?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Was it a call from a Moscow number?' Nagiyev turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'Moscow', pairing it with 'phone' and 'call'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiev",
      "t": "Да, вот номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, here is the number.' Nagiyev delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'number'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
